b/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37712" :question/id #uuid "d790d268-b73d-5bb7-b6c6-b72646e9b5be" :question/math-academy-id "q-37712" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{aligned}]$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37712/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37712"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40179/selection/e59b1317e710cc3efb03339d0dda62b111e50354d142ee4531e7a4057ac1ae98" :answer/id #uuid "59200cb7-debe-55ab-8fb0-6a5afb506399" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40179/selection/74f923e30bd1e60077b4941616bdd8e16717425c5c2d54b9fa80a277550d9802" :answer/id #uuid "1977e053-5aab-540e-b2fa-f0035e6d4ef3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40179/selection/3b5c09299df187455