lidate]} {:db/id "q-37840" :question/id #uuid "5c58fed1-6a3f-5273-bb50-b5deba510447" :question/math-academy-id "q-37840" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+4z \\\\ -5x+10y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37840/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37840"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "21eb175a-5efa-5c5a-bce4-605f003063de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection/5d022a88a643e97abec4d01dfce48411101475c0705a98f29f3a8e3560fb0616" :answer/id #uuid "6f5994a0-5904-5d01-85f8-530d4316947f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "370723a8-711d-5a80-9bd3-6db18810cee8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "fab39608-7666-5ff2-a3ab-12832b114b4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "07f91c3b-b331-5c98-9d95-c7956c20b91d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "52c23503-794c-51e5-b0e2-c54b8c835076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81388/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-81388/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-81388/selection/5d022a88a643e97abec4d01dfce48411101475c0705a98f29f3a8e3560fb0616" "q-81388/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-81388/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-81388/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81388" :question/id #uuid "0da1a486-f77f-5335-92da-99e45355d1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81388" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z \\\\ -6x+3y-3z \\\\ 4x-2y+2z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3-1+k \\\\ -6\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k \\\\ 4\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5+k \\\\ -15-3k \\\\ 10+2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5+k=0 \\\\ -15-3k=0 \\\\ 10+2k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81388/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81388"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "b3539cb7-00b3-52ba-8854-5cbcb5bceb73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "ad04e7cc-d439-5a9b-9ff7-e347e2befbbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "a11586be-b868-58ca-967c-bc8ab3b918fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "c209b194-5b3f-5fe7-b0db-90209be27d41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "1dc03676-6484-5064-b8c6-30bd6a5225e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b97a0fb8-fb6c-5c31-b4c3-51083350d332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140011/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-140011/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-140011/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-140011/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-140011/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-140011/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140011" :question/id #uuid "ecf2c30f-d27f-5d2d-8234-9467ffd2f974" :question/math-academy-id "q-140011" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y \\\\ 5x+10y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot k+10\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2k+12 \\\\ 5k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k+12=0 \\\\ 5k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140011/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140011"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37825"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -10x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ -10\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4k-12 \\\\ -10k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4k-12=0 \\\\ -10k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81387/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "fffe6fe8-c932-5eb7-a956-8af5640dc97e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81387/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a0fe2ebd-8bfb-5337-aa82-aa6a1eb2a5f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81387/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "3b0da769-38b2-5770-981c-2c7540747f68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81387/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "ef506197-3949-52aa-a82f-9c3143557ed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81387/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "ce9af07e-d04c-5f7d-