9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81387"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37842"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-8x+4y \\\\ 6x-3y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot k \\\\ 6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot 3-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-24+4k \\\\ 18-3k \\\\ 6-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-24+4k=0 \\\\ 18-3k=0 \\\\ 6-k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection/eb1133cadf7aab1d78f5cf7a1f522e78077f4d8deb92b85390ed168e4dfc061d" :answer/id #uuid "b0d6986a-9d71-5a28-beaf-84c8de102059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection/333c80ef142ee9abbe971ab6801d660487baed953a625d17acad4449d2417057" :answer/id #uuid "a4c70a6c-aa06-5505-bb49-ae51629b519d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection/239a05d0bb12535f39206ffa861a98ef796b74b84ada08a399df9a130d2b4b19" :answer/id #uuid "4fd26c3d-f29f-5214-b731-5e5d8e0de1d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection/4a5f2b069f74ec3572ac9756a5f00bcd8b68864d5be547263b7aaaeeadcb33e7" :answer/id #uuid "406349f8-4225-5116-b8a8-17a591fc66b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection/f0e252979ed15b28fd5916a1f01356fc71dbf88fc6598d754c9917a8e5089655" :answer/id #uuid "dd5e7abf-1e33-5f75-8e62-99bda363d6be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea809e15-d057-5f6c-80c8-08af76a83790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37827/selection/eb1133cadf7aab1d78f5cf7a1f522e78077f4d8deb92b85390ed168e4dfc061d" :answer-field/choices ["q-37827/selection/eb1133cadf7aab1d78f5cf7a1f522e78077f4d8deb92b85390ed168e4dfc061d" "q-37827/selection/333c80ef142ee9abbe971ab6801d660487baed953a625d17acad4449d2417057" "q-37827/selection/239a05d0bb12535f39206ffa861a98ef796b74b84ada08a399df9a130d2b4b19" "q-37827/selection/4a5f2b069f74ec3572ac9756a5f00bcd8b68864d5be547263b7aaaeeadcb33e7" "q-37827/selection/f0e252979ed15b28fd5916a1f01356fc71dbf88fc6598d754c9917a8e5089655"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37827" :question/id #uuid "16af1d3a-241b-5541-b15e-81549dff6d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-37827" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37827/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125054 :question/answer-fields 17592186123346] {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/3fdfd71be9be309c8b0d4a8b5f0a27efda5b9c60915441e7830e8b9cb65129f4" :answer/id #uuid "9ec5b23f-98d8-5922-987f-5c541ae97701" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/b58ec9669786488066aed9e109c21b7630098b67020f6068b7896d97ec6cdb4b" :answer/id #uuid "1e70861a-4ab3-5b24-9756-602cb23ed696" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/41a1587fdde7280b6d860128d9da2c68d441a1fbfbca9e752e94523c62f8db16" :answer/id #uuid "19ddfbac-b416-5338-89fb-4b3fabad153e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/3afe24fb0a77a7561dee9238138cc6c671b5c0fbd22320c6e0cdf491f8355c95" :answer/id #uuid "0bc5d5c4-9f8d-5f55-a6f3-671c7cc8f93d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/4f16e8246b932b0f8f7d2c38d9a38d10f0d674f639027309ccb1ea199787aecb" :answer/id #uuid "2ca56609-b7ea-53d4-8820-0607311a31c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b803b6c-83a0-5ac0-9349-4eb571ebadd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/b58ec9669786488066aed9e109c21b7630098b67020f6068b7896d97ec6cdb4b" :answer-field/choices ["q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/3fdfd71be9be309c8b0d4a8b5f0a27efda5b9c60915441e7830e8b9cb65129f4" "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/b58ec9669786488066aed9e109c21b7630098b67020f6068b7896d97ec6cdb4b" "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/41a1587fdde7280b6d860128d9da2c68d441a1fbfbca9e752e94523c62f8db16" "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/3afe24fb0a77a7561dee9238138cc6c671b5c0fbd22320c6e0cdf491f8355c95" "q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4/4f16e8246b932b0f8f7d2c38d9a38d10f0d674f639027309ccb1ea199787aecb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38719"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ From the third equation, we obtain $z=0$ and from the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38719/selection/ma-v1/c8cd1ce0e440130d03ee9180435deb0476b3a4a9e0e5cc820fd3c85694698ec4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection/4e0c847f7ac76b7ca11f25a5d6df01b04c62fd7268be753dc15c7f8325c318eb" :answer/id #uuid "a5ff6a0f-308e-51f6-8b78-49c8d4d3caba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection/de3b684342615eb42bd3fd791082af53f5ed14a4c85c1274c82199581db3ef5e" :answer/id #uuid "fbae4e9c-55e6-52ba-9679-c78aee49bb46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection/410e2dc125509aee85f6bb48fa5e5495ef0e380ffb00773fbc223639c4077898" :answer/id #uuid "8dcb9938-d9b7-58da-adb1-ab15e25eaf2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection/43302fc5fb707030f40212d8c4d09f51198eefbd417f1e4b1248861c1969b11b" :answer/id #uuid "c93018e8-2e15-57ba-9281-d4c850250bbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection/2f05b45e93be56ad2633f42704ce90bf93b6d876be7ee43dc16e2fe54b7d6a35" :answer/id #uuid "6a31c458-a83e-5857-947f-c95f0fc6bc66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad01c371-8dc5-5e30-933f-bf89de213b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40175/selection/2f05b45e93be56ad2633f42704ce90bf93b6d876be7ee43dc16e2fe54b7d6a35" :answer-field/choices ["q-40175/selection/4e0c847f7ac76b7ca11f25a5d6df01b04c62fd7268be753dc15c7f8325c318eb" "q-40175/selection/de3b684342615eb42bd3fd791082af53f5ed14a4c85c1274c82199581db3ef5e" "q-40175/selection/410e2dc125509aee85f6bb48fa5e5495ef0e380ffb00773fbc223639c4077898" "q-40175/selection/43302fc5fb707030f40212d8c4d09f51198eefbd417f1e4b1248861c1969b11b" "q-40175/selection/2f05b45e93be56ad2633f42704ce90bf93b6d876be7ee43dc16e2fe54b7d6a35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40175" :question/id #uuid "daa0bc39-0770-52e4-a043-5e620f02bd66" :question/math-academy-id "q-40175" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-40175/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40175"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection/0ee9d62a6542ebe2b893b4fd790c6e0d84269bf7250f595e0ba49dd4bf6e809a" :answer/id #uuid "d5f638d2-230e-5649-9702-4ba37dd22f06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection/a014d71b3fd8bc3c6def00cf3a600c1f7eea19aec671e496da5bb077cd4c53fc" :answer/id #uuid "d94445c2-7f2d-5fe4-af7e-e758cc90969c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection/7542424db492ce6030e5bca01b6d271ad7d05b7282a1371de095b75030a2af6a" :answer/id #uuid "0785d710-b055-5734-9681-fa358e674c68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection/885b8f204258ac9068289db7bb5f56f1cab483f32e59cff1f4fc9b2f482e48d9" :answer/id #uuid "6a9de56a-d495-5846-bdf6-e6e57dfa91d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection/b1cf68d489d9c0e34004ca3f623da1fde4c7d43f776941dd83ef4edd1bab11bc" :answer/id #uuid "d2c259ae-09be-5b29-8e16-8d27a64b8aa8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c50f12b1-8336-5902-96b3-28585748060a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140052/selection/885b8f204258ac9068289db7bb5f56f1cab483f32e59cff1f4fc9b2f482e48d9" :answer-field/choices ["q-140052/selection/0ee9d62a6542ebe2b893b4fd790c6e0d84269bf7250f595e0ba49dd4bf6e809a" "q-140052/selection/a014d71b3fd8bc3c6def00cf3a600c1f7eea19aec671e496da5bb077cd4c53fc" "q-140052/selection/7542424db492ce6030e5bca01b6d271ad7d05b7282a1371de095b75030a2af6a" "q-140052/selection/885b8f204258ac9068289db7bb5f56f1cab483f32e59cff1f4fc9b2f482e48d9" "q-140052/selection/b1cf68d489d9c0e34004ca3f623da1fde4c7d43f776941dd83ef4edd1bab11bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140052" :question/id #uuid "e0f0ace1-f32a-5a2a-aecf-7a206aa0a8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140052" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -6x+3y\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{6}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=y[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140052/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140052"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection/cd5c1b7ef53007b1015ebc08b2a5718aa7d325cb9bc2a62753bb185223edea1a" :answer/id #uuid "9422f8a8-298a-51a2-af93-86f2a70b237d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection/44abe81ef3938f43b8520877b95120d9cdb804692a5a3178a9e0db37792d4f29" :answer/id #uuid "0a478823-f92d-5b58-85d0-8173776be37a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection/265c5ffd5dee9491c1d2527eaad643ed0ad309066c597edfd9a85f4f55685c05" :answer/id #uuid "7228404a-bc96-527b-9b09-86de3fcb8187" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection/c58a506fe41aa3d0619b7c332007152b5c715307520b8604f85086546dd8278c" :answer/id #uuid "860ec080-cf32-53d9-bd83-25879e914bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection/8290a15cfa6cb7c0d07e9f329d81975e68baa3cf93f82b7ec8666555436966c2" :answer/id #uuid "0183b795-a839-508b-b621-24fb98fb889c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1dc666-15ab-5a30-bbcb-a11ba3f1e0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37828/selection/265c5ffd5dee9491c1d2527eaad643ed0ad309066c597edfd9a85f4f55685c05" :answer-field/choices ["q-37828/selection/cd5c1b7ef53007b1015ebc08b2a5718aa7d325cb9bc2a62753bb185223edea1a" "q-37828/selection/44abe81ef3938f43b8520877b95120d9cdb804692a5a3178a9e0db37792d4f29" "q-37828/selection/265c5ffd5dee9491c1d2527eaad643ed0ad309066c597edfd9a85f4f55685c05" "q-37828/selection/c58a506fe41aa3d0619b7c332007152b5c715307520b8604f85086546dd8278c" "q-37828/selection/8290a15cfa6cb7c0d07e9f329d81975e68baa3cf93f82b7ec8666555436966c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37828" :question/id #uuid "34778fc0-93c4-5c13-b7d6-916e1d349a22" :question/math-academy-id "q-37828" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37828/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37828"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d875f465-cfd2-5c7f-a16b-3e10dea63e38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "d7a50f54-acbd-5b80-b354-e912ee942dcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "1b8d0205-fa55-56ea-b853-d548aa774efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "ee6d33b5-ee79-527b-8d9c-3266e46600d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "bddb983c-08ba-551d-99b1-e0a2771b0b00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "069f5a2d-d6d2-5df1-9f18-2fb82996d311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81391/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-81391/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-81391/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-81391/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-81391/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-81391/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81391" :question/id #uuid "29b1b7b0-589e-51c0-afb6-47caa8419215" :question/math-academy-id "q-81391" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 16y-6x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3=2\\cdot 1-3=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81391/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81391"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "bdee2855-8389-5e84-86f8-17fc4b46cef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "271e6be6-3260-5be5-9d7e-9e2f3182ba69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "cc05843d-7476-5f8d-a8e5-583921f5c949" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8294ea1e-4d25-58b2-9e8c-39f820bf6fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "af54e08d-d72d-5101-b7f1-d77e94c2779b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12917101-dcbd-5d98-9e72-3ca7ccf37c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81392/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-81392/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-81392/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-81392/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-81392/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-81392/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81392" :question/id #uuid "5ea31c94-867a-5e75-a463-fbd5411648eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-81392" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81392/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81392"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "36f6189c-fa3e-5019-b7f9-840275cb3e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "587b651e-0212-52f2-b9b2-1b0fb7303bb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "d7c6f8e7-04b2-5f53-9637-7d9f26ce5b8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8535d721-30bc-5c23-b914-4877120b4ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c0adc9c0-8c90-5eee-bf1c-7d91d4355d42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a81405d3-af48-553f-bb05-8f72bc410a96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80723/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-80723/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-80723/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-80723/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-80723/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-80723/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80723" :question/id #uuid "b1375a76-1197-5dc8-9cbc-a9ed71a37739" :question/math-academy-id "q-80723" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the third one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80723/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "dfee4b62-4bb8-5a15-81cd-f73597143032" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e6c6893e-4531-54a0-9fc6-b8ac2a6542ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "925e75f7-1cdf-5355-9487-ba0f0675662f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "2f403c5e-908a-596d-9f67-ee9f51dfc29c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "47879b2a-1019-53dd-b7c5-9b0909f0323a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e944f9a-36eb-52f9-a6e4-6e350e3163ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40177/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-40177/selection/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" "q-40177/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-40177/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-40177/selection/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" "q-40177/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40177" :question/id #uuid "554c3563-4c0d-5d18-8e54-b01c022c59b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40177" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}],$ find $9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4=9\\cdot 0+4=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-40177/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38676"] :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-7y \\\\ 14y-10x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1=3\\cdot 1+1=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5616"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5441"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5439"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5440"] :question/problem "Given the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
